 would be logical. If the metrics show it\'s down, then looking into the pod\'s logs would help find the root cause. Also, checking the dependency graph might show if other services are dependent on media-mongodb, which could affect the overall application. But since the user hasn\'t specified any particular error, maybe starting with the metrics for media-mongodb makes sense. Alternatively, using get_traces for services that might be affected by media-mongodb\'s status. But first, confirming if media-mongodb is actually down via metrics would be the next step.\n</think>\n\nget_metrics  \n"up{job=\\"media-mongodb\\"}"  \nThe previous deployment check showed media-mongodb has 0/1 available replicas. To confirm if the service is actually down or just not reporting metrics, I need to query its Prometheus metrics directly. This will validate whether the service is unreachable, crashing, or simply not emitting metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 410, 'prompt_tokens': 2462, 'total_tokens': 2872, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-71ea-7e82-b797-c27cb499bd73-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2462, 'output_tokens': 410, 'total_tokens': 2872}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e4f537a4-637c-4da3-a633-8541f4ee4429'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their social network application, which consists of many microservices. They mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. \n\nFirst, they started by using the get_services tool to list all services in Grafana, which gives an overview of the components. Then, they checked metrics for the user-service using get_metrics, but got an empty result, indicating possible issues. Next, they used kubectl to check deployments and found that media-mongodb has 0/1 available replicas, meaning it\'s not running as expected.\n\nNow, the user is focusing on media-mongodb. The next step is to check the metrics for media-mongodb directly using get_metrics with the query "up{job=\\"media-mongodb\\"}". The response will show if the service is up or down. If the metric is 0, it confirms the service isn\'t running. If there\'s no data, maybe the metrics aren\'t being collected properly. \n\nAfter getting the metrics, if media-mongodb is indeed down, they should check the pods associated with it. Using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" can show if the pod is in a crash loop or has errors. If the pod is not running, checking the logs with "kubectl logs" might reveal the root cause, like configuration errors or dependency issues. \n\nThey should also look into the deployment configuration for media-mongodb to ensure the correct number of replicas and check for any rollout issues. If the problem persists, looking at the service\'s dependencies via get_dependency_graph could help identify if other services are affecting it. \n\nOverall, the key steps are verifying the service\'s status through metrics, checking pod health, reviewing deployment settings, and examining logs for detailed error messages. This approach should help pinpoint whether the issue is with the service itself, its configuration, or external factors.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "up{job=\\"media-mongodb\\"}"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a8566f1f9e885cd5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 422, 'prompt_tokens': 3437, 'total_tokens': 3859, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-8560-74a0-a0a3-155099ee14f3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="media-mongodb"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a8566f1f9e885cd5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3437, 'output_tokens': 422, 'total_tokens': 3859}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='233a6ea1-db8d-410f-8f62-e326338e7720', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a8566f1f9e885cd5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='395b04f5-39e0-447a-b10e-36ee97d6395e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb service isn\'t available. From the previous steps, we checked the deployments and saw that media-mongodb has 0/1 available replicas. Then, we tried to get metrics for media-mongodb using Prometheus, but the result was empty, meaning no metrics were found.\n\nSo, the next step is to figure out why the media-mongodb isn\'t running. Since the deployment shows it\'s not available, maybe the pod isn\'t running at all. The user already checked the deployments, but maybe they need to look at the actual pods. Using kubectl to get the pods in the social-network namespace would show if the media-mongodb pod exists and its status. If the pod isn\'t there, maybe there was an error in deployment or the image is faulty. Alternatively, if the pod is running but in a crash loop, checking the logs would help. So, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to check the pod status. If that doesn\'t show the pod, then check the deployment details or events for more info. But first, confirm if the pod exists.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nThe media-mongodb deployment shows 0/1 available replicas, but we need to verify if the pod is actually running or if it\'s in a crash loop, pending state, or non-existent. Checking the pod list directly will reveal its current status, including any errors or restarts, which is critical to diagnosing the root cause.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 336, 'prompt_tokens': 2636, 'total_tokens': 2972, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-9a02-7842-9aa2-729e170a25eb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2636, 'output_tokens': 336, 'total_tokens': 2972}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5f0ef53a-5e53-4ce1-8cba-b44bc784e118'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the media-mongodb service in their social network application. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments and saw that media-mongodb has 0/1 available replicas. Then they queried Prometheus metrics for media-mongodb, but got an empty result, indicating that the service might not be up or not emitting metrics.\n\nNow, the user is suggesting to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" again. Wait, they already did that earlier, and the output showed media-mongodb as 0/1. But maybe they want to check the pods again to see if there\'s any change or more details. However, the previous output didn\'t show the pod name, so maybe the pod isn\'t listed because it\'s in a crash loop or pending. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user wants to check the pod\'s logs to see why it\'s not running. But the available tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can run commands like logs. Wait, the user\'s last tool call was "kubectl get pods -n social-network", and the response included media-mongodb as 0/1. But the user is asking again. Maybe they need to check the pod\'s status in more detail. \n\nWait, the user might not have seen the pod name. Let me check the previous response. The deployments list showed media-mongodb as 0/1, but the pods list might show the actual pod name. If the pod is not running, the "kubectl get pods" would list it as CrashLoopBackOff or something similar. So, the user is trying to get the pod list again to see the exact status. \n\nBut the user already ran "kubectl get pods" earlier. Maybe the user wants to check the logs of the specific pod. However, the available command is "kubectl logs" which requires the pod name. Since the previous "get pods" didn\'t show the pod name (maybe it\'s not listed because it\'s in a crash loop), the user might need to use "kubectl describe pod" to get more details. But the available commands include "kubectl describe". \n\nWait, the available commands for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd include "kubectl describe". So maybe the user should use "kubectl describe pod <pod-name>" to get more information. But first, they need the pod name. Alternatively, maybe the user can use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" again to get the pod name. \n\nWait, in the previous response, when the user ran "kubectl get pods -n social-network", the output didn\'t include the pod name for media-mongodb. Wait, no, the output from the previous tool call was a list of deployments, not pods. Wait, the user first ran "kubectl get deployments" and then "kubectl get pods". Wait, looking back, the user first called "kubectl get deployments" and then "kubectl get pods". The "kubectl get pods" response included media-mongodb as 0/1, but the actual pod name might not be listed. \n\nWait, the user\'s previous tool call was "kubectl get deployments -n social-network", which showed media-mongodb as 0/1. Then, the user called "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and the response included media-mongodb as 0/1. Wait, no, the "kubectl get pods" command would list the pods, not deployments. Wait, maybe there was confusion between deployments and pods. \n\nWait, the user first called "kubectl get deployments" and saw media-mongodb with 0/1. Then, they called "kubectl get pods" to check the actual pods. The response from "kubectl get pods" would list the pods, including the media-mongodb pod. If the pod is not running, it would show as CrashLoopBackOff or Pending. \n\nBut in the previous response, the user\'s tool call was "kubectl get deployments" and then "kubectl get pods". The "kubectl get pods" response included media-mongodb as 0/1, but that\'s part of the deployment status. Wait, no, the deployment status shows the number of available replicas, but the actual pod status would be in the pods list. \n\nSo, the user is trying to get the pods list again to see the exact status. However, the previous response might not have included the pod name. Therefore, the user needs to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" again to get the pod names and their statuses. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should check the logs of the pod if it\'s running. But since the pod is not available, they need to first check the pod\'s status. So, the next step is to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" again to confirm the pod\'s status and name. \n\nWait, but the user already did that. Maybe the user needs to check the logs of the specific pod. However, without the pod name, they can\'t do that. So, the next step is to get the pod name from the "kubectl get pods" command. \n\nBut the user\'s last tool call was "kubectl get pods -n social-network", and the response included media-mongodb as 0/1. Wait, no, the response from "kubectl get pods" would list the pods, not the deployments. The deployments are separate. So, the user might have confused deployments with pods. \n\nIn any case, the user is trying to find out why media-mongodb is not available. The next step is to check the pod\'s status, which requires running "kubectl get pods" again to get the pod name and its status. If the pod is not running, they can then check the logs or describe the pod for more details.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-983e437eb70d73b1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1225, 'prompt_tokens': 3626, 'total_tokens': 4851, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-a97a-7881-9a13-31881e838c33-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-983e437eb70d73b1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3626, 'output_tokens': 1225, 'total_tokens': 4851}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS              RESTARTS   AGE\ncompose-post-service-886c98b48-jrnw2      1/1     Running             0          3m12s\nhome-timeline-redis-5cdbd67f7c-kg655      1/1     Running             0          3m14s\nhome-timeline-service-5d6bb5597d-kdc7n    1/1     Running             0          3m14s\njaeger-68c668dfbc-qhg56                   1/1     Running             0          3m13s\nmedia-frontend-f49684d4c-mcbtc            1/1     Running             0          3m14s\nmedia-memcached-8bdff88f6-nqcb4           1/1     Running             0          3m14s\nmedi