e a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='866d0850-a55a-4c88-984c-b232d372dbff'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the media-mongodb service in their social network application. From the previous steps, we know that the media-mongodb deployment has 0/1 available replicas, and the Prometheus metrics query for "up{job=\\"media-mongodb\\"}" returned an empty result. Also, when checking the pods in the social-network namespace, the pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w is in a "ContainerCreating" state.\n\nSo, the next step is to figure out why the media-mongodb pod isn\'t running. The pod is in ContainerCreating, which usually means that the container is being created but hasn\'t started yet. Possible reasons could be issues with the container image, problems with the pod\'s configuration, or resource constraints. \n\nSince the pod is in ContainerCreating, maybe the container image isn\'t pulling correctly. I should check the logs of the pod to see if there are any errors during the creation process. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool can be used to get the logs. The command would be "kubectl logs media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network". \n\nAlternatively, if the logs aren\'t available yet, maybe the pod hasn\'t started logging, or there\'s an issue with the container itself. Another possibility is that the pod is stuck in ContainerCreating due to a missing volume or a configuration error. Checking the pod\'s details with "kubectl describe pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network" could provide more information about the status and any events that occurred during the creation.\n\nBut since the user has a limit of 20 rounds, and they\'ve already used several commands, it\'s efficient to first check the logs. If the logs show an error, that would point to the root cause. If not, then describing the pod would give more details. However, the next step should be to get the logs to see if there\'s an immediate issue with the container startup.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network"  \nThe media-mongodb pod is in "ContainerCreating" state, indicating a potential issue during container initialization. Checking the logs will reveal specific errors (e.g., image pull failures, configuration issues, or startup problems) preventing the container from transitioning to "Running" status. This is critical to identify the exact cause of the pod\'s stalled state.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 543, 'prompt_tokens': 3803, 'total_tokens': 4346, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-e4d2-7ce0-ba28-83cac5bbd912-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3803, 'output_tokens': 543, 'total_tokens': 4346}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1f1b2290-934f-48e4-a757-b2962163b0bd'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod is not running. They\'ve already checked the deployments and pods, and the pod is in ContainerCreating state. The next step is to check the logs to find out why the container isn\'t starting.\n\nThe available tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, and the command would be to get the logs of the media-mongodb pod. But wait, the pod is in ContainerCreating, so maybe the logs aren\'t available yet. Or maybe the container hasn\'t started at all. However, sometimes even in ContainerCreating, you can get logs from the init containers or the main container if it\'s failing during startup.\n\nThe user\'s previous attempt to get metrics for media-mongodb showed no up{job="media-mongodb"}, which suggests the service isn\'t running or not reporting metrics. So the logs are the next logical step. If the logs show an error, like an image pull error, misconfigured environment variables, or a failed health check, that would point to the root cause.\n\nAlternatively, if the logs aren\'t available, maybe the pod hasn\'t started the container yet, and the logs would be empty or show the initial setup steps. But the user needs to check the logs to get more details. So the correct tool call here is to execute kubectl logs on the media-mongodb pod to retrieve any error messages or startup issues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-95769f75eaed89f1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 339, 'prompt_tokens': 4811, 'total_tokens': 5150, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7b-fe69-7682-bec3-b4fd16986ca4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-95769f75eaed89f1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4811, 'output_tokens': 339, 'total_tokens': 5150}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (BadRequest): container "media-mongodb" in pod "media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w" is waiting to start: ContainerCreating\n', id='aaa9ece8-0db4-492a-a684-897bd4e5b85f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-95769f75eaed89f1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e60bf128-6ab0-425a-865d-11b9bbfcfdbf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod is in ContainerCreating state. They tried to get the logs, but the command was rejected because the container is still in the process of being created. So, logs might not be available yet.\n\nHmm, what\'s the next step? Well, maybe check the events related to the pod. The kubectl describe pod command can show detailed information about the pod\'s status, including any events that might explain why it\'s stuck. Events often have error messages or warnings that can point to the root cause, like image pull errors, storage issues, or resource constraints.\n\nSo, the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl describe pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network". This will give detailed information about the pod\'s current state and any events that have occurred. Looking at the events can help identify if there\'s a problem with the container image, volumes, or other dependencies. That makes sense. Let\'s go with that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network"  \nThe pod is in "ContainerCreating" state, and logs are not yet available. Using `kubectl describe pod` will provide detailed status, events, and reasons for the container creation failure (e.g., image pull errors, volume mounting issues, or resource constraints). This is the most direct way to diagnose the root cause of the stalled container.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 333, 'prompt_tokens': 4064, 'total_tokens': 4397, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7c-1121-7301-829c-c8f1cce997be-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4064, 'output_tokens': 333, 'total_tokens': 4397}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='40ea22da-9e6f-4d28-9dba-08411a34e399'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod is in a ContainerCreating state. They\'ve already checked the deployments and saw that it\'s at 0/1 available. The kubectl get pods shows the pod is in ContainerCreating, and trying to get logs didn\'t work. So the next step is to use kubectl describe pod to get more details.\n\nThe describe command will show events related to the pod, which can indicate why the container isn\'t starting. Common issues might be image pull errors, missing secrets, or problems with the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) if the MongoDB pod needs storage. Since MongoDB is a stateful service, it\'s possible there\'s an issue with the storage configuration. The describe output should mention if there are any events like "Failed to pull image" or "Failed to mount volumes." \n\nLooking at the previous steps, the user hasn\'t checked the PVCs yet. Maybe the PVC for media-mongodb isn\'t properly provisioned, leading to the container failing to start. Alternatively, the MongoDB image might not be accessible, or there could be resource limits preventing the container from starting. The describe command\'s events section would clarify these points. Once the root cause is identified, the user can take corrective action, like fixing the PVC, correcting the image name, or adjusting resource limits.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-99ea4a5de72363f8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 327, 'prompt_tokens': 5073, 'total_tokens': 5400, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7c-20c6-7e03-9c50-27d477625ed2-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-99ea4a5de72363f8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5073, 'output_tokens': 327, 'total_tokens': 5400}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w\nNamespace:        social-network\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.31\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 15:47:20 +0000\nLabels:           app=media-mongodb\n                  pod-template-hash=85f4f7f55\n                  service=media-mongodb\nAnnotations:      <none>\nStatus:           Pending\nIP:               \nIPs:              <none>\nControlled By:    ReplicaSet/media-mongodb-85f4f7f55\nContainers:\n  media-mongodb:\n    Container ID:  \n    Image:         docker.io/library/mongo:4.4.6\n    Image ID:      \n    Port:          27017/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Args:\n      --config\n      /social-network-microservices/config/mongod.conf\n    State:          Waiting\n      Reason:       ContainerCreating\n    Ready:          False\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Environment:    <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /social-network-microservices/config/mongod.conf from media-mongodb-config (rw,path="mongod.conf")\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-gfkxl (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   False \n  Initialized                 True \n  Ready                       False \n  ContainersReady             False \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  media-mongodb-config:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      media-mongodb\n    Optional:  false\n  kube-api-access-gfkxl:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   BestEffort\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason       Age                From               Message\n  ----     ------       ----               ----               -------\n  Normal   Scheduled    84s                default-scheduler  Successfully assigned social-network/media-mongodb-85f4f7f55-lf84w to node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Warning  FailedMount  20s (x8 over 84s)  kubelet            MountVolume.SetUp failed for volume "media-mongodb-config" : configmap "media-mongodb" not found\n', id='4ed9179d-143d-406f-a0e9-27e51d27e9eb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-99ea4a5de72363f8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9eaab79a-3e0d-4333-8c9f-34dcda9c55fb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod is in a ContainerCreating state. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, metrics, and logs. The key issue here is the "FailedMount" event mentioned in the describe pod output. The error says that the configmap "media-mongodb" is not found. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check if the ConfigMap named "media-mongodb" exists in the social-network namespace. If it doesn\'t, that\'s why the volume mount is failing. The tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network". \n\nBy running this command, they can confirm whether the ConfigMap is present. If it\'s missing, that\'s the root cause. If it exists, maybe there\'s another issue. But given the error message, the ConfigMap isn\'t found, so creating it or checking its existence is crucial. This will help pinpoint the problem accurately.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network"  \nThe pod\'s `FailedMount` event indicates the ConfigMap `media-mongodb` is missing. This command will confirm whether the ConfigMap exists in the namespace, directly addressing the root cause of the volume mounting failure preventing the container from starting.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 288, 'prompt_tokens': 4937, 'total_tokens': 5225, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7c-33ae-7463-84b0-d460fdebc4fd-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4937, 'output_tokens': 288, 'total_tokens': 5225}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='58d9dac8-e82a-46e1-9845-e81a970b960b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod isn\'t starting. From the previous steps, we found that the pod is in a ContainerCreating state, and the describe pod output showed a FailedMount event because the ConfigMap "media-mongodb" wasn\'t found. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check if that ConfigMap actually exists in the social-network namespace. The user suggested using kubectl to get the ConfigMap. If the ConfigMap doesn\'t exist, that\'s the root cause. If it does, maybe there\'s a typo or a different issue.\n\nI need to call the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command "kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network". This will retrieve the ConfigMap details. If it\'s not found, the output will indicate that, confirming the problem. If it is found, then there might be another issue, but at least we can rule out the ConfigMap\'s existence as the problem. \n\nThis step is crucial because the missing ConfigMap is preventing the volume from mounting, which is why the container can\'t start. By verifying the ConfigMap\'s presence, we can pinpoint the exact cause and proceed accordingly.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bf60bdc394fcc1b8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 286, 'prompt_tokens': 5912, 'total_tokens': 6198, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7c-418e-7fa2-9f1a-0b4e00438901-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bf60bdc394fcc1b8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5912, 'output_tokens': 286, 'total_tokens': 6198}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): configmaps "media-mongodb" not found\n', id='c14976ac-76e7-420d-ad24-7c32e7a6ee6d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bf60bdc394fcc1b8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b9b3baba-852f-4b02-8165-e8803698f51c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the media-mongodb pod isn\'t starting. The previous steps showed that the pod is in a ContainerCreating state, and the describe pod output mentioned a FailedMount event because the ConfigMap "media-mongodb" wasn\'t found. The user tried to check the ConfigMap with kubectl get configmap media-mongodb -n social-network, but it returned an error saying the configmap wasn\'t found.\n\nSo, the next step is to figure out why the ConfigMap is missing. Maybe it wasn\'t created at all. The user needs to check if the ConfigMap exists in the namespace. Since the command to get the ConfigMap failed, the next logical step is to check if the ConfigMap was ever created. If it\'s not present, then the issue is likely that the ConfigMap wasn\'t created, possibly due to a typo in the name, a missing deployment or configmap resource, or an error during setup.\n\nThe available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can be used to check for the existence of the ConfigMap. Alternatively, maybe checking the deployments or other resources related to media-mongodb could help. But since the ConfigMap is specifically mentioned in the error, the focus should remain there. The user should verify if the ConfigMap was created. If it\'s not there, then the root cause is the absence of the ConfigMap, which is required for the pod to mount the configuration file. \n\nAnother possibility is that the ConfigMap was created but in a different namespace. However, the error message specifies the namespace as social-network, so that\'s less likely. The user should confirm the existence of the ConfigMap in the correct namespace. If it\'s not there, then the next step would be to check the deployment or any other resources that might be responsible for creating the ConfigMap. But given the tools available, the immediate action is to check for the ConfigMap\'s existence again, perhaps with a more detailed command to list all ConfigMaps in the namespace to see if any similar names exist. However, the user can only use the provided tools. \n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. So, they can run "kubectl get configmap -n social-network" to list all ConfigMaps in the namespace. That would help confirm if the media-mongodb ConfigMap is missing or if there\'s a typo. Alternatively, maybe the ConfigMap was created with a different name. So, the next tool call should be to list all ConfigMaps in the social-network namespace to check for the presence of the media-mongodb ConfigMap. If it\'s not there, then the root cause is the missing ConfigMap. If it is there, maybe there\'s another issue. But given the previous error, it\'s likely missing. So, the user should check all ConfigMaps to confirm.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get configmap -n social-network"  \nThe `FailedMount` event explicitly states the ConfigMap `media-mongodb` is missing. Listing all ConfigMaps in the namespace will confirm whether this resource exists or if there was a typo, misconfiguration, or deletion. This directly addresses the root cause of the volume mounting failure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 665, 'prompt_tokens': 5126, 'total_tokens': 5791, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc7c-5248-79a0-be94-fc44f3